module lib/hf/eri_store
  Storage for the electron repulsion integrals `(ij|kl)`, one copy per
  symmetry class. The integral does not change when i and j swap, when k and
  l swap, or when the pair ij trades places with the pair kl, so the eight
  index orders of one value share one slot of a packed vector ("packed-8").
  The module numbers the pairs, the slots, and reads and writes them.

es_npair  @ae57469e
  why   The packed length and every loop over pairs start from this count.
  n   how many orbitals (basis functions)

es_len8  @f65d0767
  why   Every ERI member allocates its result with this length, and the
        sweep drivers compare that many numbers
        [test "the packed lengths for 19 and 13 orbitals are 18145 and 4186"].
  n   how many orbitals (basis functions)
  np  how many index pairs `i >= j` there are

es_pair  @8b9ba817
  why   The pairs `i >= j` numbered in order give each unordered pair one
        index, and the same numbering applied to two pair indices gives each
        integral's symmetry class one slot.
  i     one orbital index
  j     the other

es_index8  @a66dddaf
  why   Every reader of a packed vector goes through this, so all eight orders
        of an integral land on the same slot
        [test "the 8 index orders share one slot, and the slots cover 0 .. len-1 once"].
  i     the first orbital of the bra pair
  j     the second orbital of the bra pair
  k     the first orbital of the ket pair
  l     the second orbital of the ket pair
  ij    the bra pair's index
  kl    the ket pair's index

es_get  @b7256b64
  Gp    the packed vector, `[es_len8(n)]`
  i     the first orbital of the bra pair
  j     the second orbital of the bra pair
  k     the first orbital of the ket pair
  l     the second orbital of the ket pair

es_pack8  @26eaa807
  why   Tests and reference data start from dense tensors; packing reads one
        canonical order per class and so needs the input to be symmetric
        [test "pack and unpack round-trip exactly on a random 8-fold-symmetric tensor, n = 7"].
  G     the dense tensor, `[n, n, n, n]`
  n     how many orbitals
  Gp    the packed vector being filled
  i     the first bra orbital, the larger of the pair
  j     the second bra orbital
  ij    the bra pair's index
  k     the first ket orbital, no larger than i
  l     the second ket orbital
  kl    the ket pair's index; only `kl <= ij` is stored

es_unpack8  @45db48b7
  why   For comparing with dense references and for code that indexes
        `[i, j, k, l]` directly.
  Gp    the packed vector
  n     how many orbitals
  G     the dense view being filled
  i     the first orbital
  j     the second orbital
  ij    the bra pair's index
  k     the third orbital
  l     the fourth orbital

es_rng  @b1b66aac
  why   The reference data for large bases ships as checksums and seeded
        samples, and the weights and sample positions must be the same numbers
        the Python generator drew; Park-Miller is exact in doubles and easy to
        reproduce in Python
        [test "checksums and sample indices match the Python reproduction of the generator"].
  st    the generator's state, `st[0]`, advanced in place

es_checksums  @22d0b4ac
  Gp    the packed vector
  seed  the generator's starting state
  st    the generator's state
  w     the weights, one per slot, drawn in slot order
  k     a slot

es_sample_index  @377e3ccb
  why   The reference file holds values only at these positions, so both sides
        must draw the same ones.
  len8  the packed length the positions fall in
  seed  the generator's starting state
  m     how many positions to draw
  st    the generator's state
  idx   the positions, `[m]`
  k     the draw being made

es_sample_gap  @26422dd6
  Gp     the packed vector computed here
  idx    the sampled positions
  vals   the reference values at those positions

es_sym_tensor  @37258177
  n     how many orbitals
  seed  the generator's starting state
  st    the generator's state
  x     one random number per orbital, for the symmetric sums
  y     another per orbital, for the symmetric products
  i     the first orbital
  j     the second orbital
  p     the bra pair's value, the same for (i, j) and (j, i)
  k     the third orbital
  l     the fourth orbital
  q     the ket pair's value, the same for (k, l) and (l, k)
  G     the tensor, `[n, n, n, n]`

es_sym_defect  @848b15f2
  why   Confirms that `es_sym_tensor` is symmetric before it is used to test
        the packing.
  G      the dense tensor
  n      how many orbitals
  worst  the largest disagreement so far
  i      the first index
  j      the second index
  k      the third index
  l      the fourth index
  v      the entry in the order `[i, j, k, l]`
